.Dd November 15, 2026
.Dt ACPI_ASUS_HWM 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm acpi_asus_hwm
.Nd ASUS motherboard fan monitoring and control
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To load the driver as a
module at boot time, place the following line in
.Xr loader.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
acpi_asus_hwm_load="YES"
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver provides fan speed monitoring and fan duty cycle control for ASUS
desktop motherboards with a Nuvoton NCT6791D, NCT6792D, NCT6793D, NCT6795D,
NCT6796D, NCT6797D, NCT6798D or NCT6799D Super I/O chip.
.Pp
On these boards the firmware owns the Super I/O ports, but provides access
to the hardware monitor through the
.Dq WMBD
method of an ACPI WMI device whose
.Dv _UID
is
.Dq AsusMbSwInterface
or
.Dq ASUSWMI .
The driver attaches as a child of
.Xr acpi_wmi 4
on that device, and performs all register accesses through the firmware.
.Pp
By default every fan follows the curve configured in the firmware setup
utility.
A fan can be switched to manual control, after which its duty cycle is
set through
.Xr sysctl 8 ,
or through the
.Xr pwmc 4
interface described below.
When the driver is unloaded, or the system shuts down or suspends, every
fan under manual control is handed back to the firmware with the settings
it had when the driver attached.
After a resume, all fans are under firmware control.
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
The following variables are available for each fan header
.Ar N ,
starting at 1:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va dev.acpi_asus_hwm.0.fan Ns Ar N Ns Va .rpm
Fan speed in revolutions per minute (read-only).
It reads 0 when nothing is connected to the header, and when the fan has
stopped.
.It Va dev.acpi_asus_hwm.0.fan Ns Ar N Ns Va .manual
Set to 1 to put the fan under manual control, or 0 to return it to the
firmware curve.
Switching to manual control keeps the current duty cycle, so the fan speed
does not change until
.Va pwm
is written.
.It Va dev.acpi_asus_hwm.0.fan Ns Ar N Ns Va .pwm
Fan duty cycle, from 0 (off) to 255 (full speed).
Under firmware control this reports the duty cycle chosen by the firmware,
and writing it fails with
.Er EBUSY .
Under manual control it can be written.
.El
.Pp
Writable variables are only accessible to the superuser.
.Sh PWM INTERFACE
The driver registers a
.Xr pwmbus 9
controller with one channel per fan header, so each fan also appears as a
.Xr pwmc 4
device that
.Xr pwm 8
and daemons such as
.Nm fand
.Pq Pa sysutils/fand
can drive.
Channel
.Ar N Ns \-1
controls fan
.Ar N .
.Pp
The channel state maps onto the fan as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "duty, period"
.It enable
Enabling the channel puts the fan under manual control, as
.Va manual Ns =1
does.
Disabling it returns the fan to the firmware curve.
A disabled channel never stops the fan.
.It "duty, period"
The duty cycle is scaled to the 8-bit fan duty register relative to the
period, so only the ratio matters.
The default period is 40000 ns (25 kHz, the PWM fan standard).
A period of 0 is rejected with
.Er EINVAL ,
because it would turn every percentage into a duty cycle of 0.
While the channel is disabled, the reported duty cycle is the one chosen by
the firmware.
.It flags
Inverted polarity is not supported and is rejected with
.Er EINVAL .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/dev/pwm/pwmcX.Y" -compact
.It Pa /dev/pwm/pwmc Ns Ar X . Ns Ar Y
.Xr pwmc 4
device for channel
.Ar Y
of PWM controller
.Ar X .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Run fan 2 at about 60% of full speed:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sysctl dev.acpi_asus_hwm.0.fan2.manual=1
sysctl dev.acpi_asus_hwm.0.fan2.pwm=153
.Ed
.Pp
Return it to the firmware curve:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sysctl dev.acpi_asus_hwm.0.fan2.manual=0
.Ed
.Pp
The same with
.Xr pwm 8 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pwm -f pwmc0.1 -E -p 40000 -d 60%
pwm -f pwmc0.1 -D
.Ed
.Pp
Let
.Nm fand
drive fan 2 from the temperature of CPU 0: 40% from 50\(deC, full speed from
75\(deC, and back to the firmware curve below 45\(deC.
In
.Xr rc.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
fand_enable="YES"
fand_sensor="dev.cpu.0.temperature"
fand_device="pwmc0.1"
fand_temp="50"
fand_off_temp="45"
fand_period="40000"
fand_duty="40%"
fand_multiplier="0.1"
fand_flags="-T 75 -P 40000 -D 100% -i 5"
.Ed
.Pp
.Va fand_multiplier
converts the tenths of a kelvin reported by
.Xr coretemp 4 .
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -diag
.It "acpi_asus_hwm0: Unsupported chip (id %#06x, chip id %#04x)"
The firmware gives access to a Super I/O chip that the driver does not
know, and the driver did not attach.
.It "acpi_asus_hwm0: Couldn't restore firmware control of fan%d"
The fan could not be handed back to the firmware.
It keeps its last manual duty cycle until the next reboot.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr acpi 4 ,
.Xr acpi_asus_wmi 4 ,
.Xr acpi_wmi 4 ,
.Xr coretemp 4 ,
.Xr pwmc 4 ,
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
.Xr sysctl.conf 5 ,
.Xr pwm 8 ,
.Xr sysctl 8 ,
.Xr pwmbus 9
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
driver first appeared as a port in 2026.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Pouria Mousavizadeh Tehrani Aq Mt pouria@FreeBSD.org
.Sh CAVEATS
Under manual control the fan runs at the requested duty cycle regardless
of temperature.
Fans differ at low duty cycles: some keep a built-in minimum speed and
ignore lower values, while others stop and report 0 RPM.
The CPU cooler, and the pump of a liquid cooler, must never be stopped:
the processor can overheat within seconds.
A stopped pump is easy to miss, because the radiator fans keep spinning
and the firmware raises their speed as the processor heats up, so the
system gets louder rather than quieter.
Check
.Va rpm
after every change, and keep the CPU temperature in view with
.Xr coretemp 4 .
.Pp
The mapping from
.Ar N
to the header names printed on the board varies between models.
.Pp
.Nm fand
does not hand the fan back when it is stopped, so the fan keeps its last
duty cycle.
Disable the channel afterwards with
.Ql pwm -f pwmc0.1 -D ,
or unload the driver.
